---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import SEO from '../components/SEO.astro';
import ForwardLink from '../components/ForwardLink.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Model Workbench';
const pageDescription =
  'Notes from the field building with AI: short practical notes on models, CADE, AI-assisted development, and human judgment.';

const noteTypes = [
  {
    title: 'AI in the work',
    text: 'What models are good at, what they are bad at, and where I have to slow the process down.',
  },
  {
    title: 'CADE lessons',
    text: 'Small observations from building and running a decision exercise under real constraints.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Build notes',
    text: 'Practical notes on prompts, workflows, review gates, handoff, and keeping output useful.',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout>
  <SEO
    slot="head"
    title={pageTitle}
    description={pageDescription}
    type="website"
  />

  <section class="workbench-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Model Workbench</p>
    <h1>Notes from the field building with AI.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      This is the place for short notes about how I actually use models: not as a magic answer machine, but as a workbench for thinking, drafting, testing, and building.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="workbench-intro">
    <h2>What belongs here</h2>
    <p>
      A Model Workbench note should be short, concrete, and useful. The target length is about 1,000 to 2,000 characters: long enough to explain one lesson, short enough to read between meetings.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tone should stay close to the work. If a note sounds like a keynote, it is probably too polished. If it explains what changed in the way I build, review, or think with AI, it belongs here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="note-grid" aria-label="Model Workbench note types">
    {noteTypes.map((item, index) => (
      <article>
        <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
        <h2>{item.title}</h2>
        <p>{item.text}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="workbench-entries" aria-label="Workbench notes">
    <p class="eyebrow">Notes</p>
    <article class="workbench-entry">
      <header class="entry-header">
        <time class="entry-date" datetime="2026-05-31">May 31, 2026</time>
        <h2>My AI Stack Is a Workbench</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="entry-body">
        <p>I don't have a favorite model. I have a workbench.</p>
        <p>Each model has a job.</p>
        <p>Claude Code is where I start when the work is still fuzzy — discovery, design, specs, audits. Codex is where I hand a spec when I want something built fast. Gemini handles deep research when I need a wide pass across a topic. NotebookLM is for working inside source material once I have it. Local models like Qwen or Hermes run on a Mac mini for lightweight tasks when I want to save tokens.</p>
        <p>The point isn't to collect tools. The point is knowing what each one is actually good for.</p>
        <p>That only comes from use. Gemini can loop. Claude Code is strong but expensive — session limits matter. Codex builds fast, but I don't trust it for design work the way I trust Claude Code. Those aren't complaints. They're operating facts.</p>
        <p>My rule: no model owns the final product.</p>
        <p>The models draft, research, build, audit, and refactor. I make the decisions — structure, direction, quality. I steer. I decide when it's right.</p>
        <p>That matters because models are persuasive. Without thread discipline, you can spend hours chasing the next recommendation. Manage tokens. Watch for drift. Return to the original intent.</p>
        <p>The workbench is valuable not because of the list of tools. It's valuable because you know which tool belongs where, when to switch, and when to stop listening.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="example-note">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example soundbite</p>
    <h2>The model is fast. That is not the same as being done.</h2>
    <p>
      A model can produce a clean-looking draft before the real problem is understood. That is useful only if I have already defined what the draft must do, what source it must obey, and what would make it unsafe or useless. Speed helps when the frame is right. When the frame is wrong, speed just makes the wrong thing arrive sooner.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="workbench-cta">
    <div>
      <h2>Back to the work</h2>
      <p>
        The notes are the workshop. CADE is the proof that the workshop can produce something usable.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ForwardLink href="/projects/cade" text="Read the CADE case study" />
  </section>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .workbench-hero {
    padding: 2rem 0 3rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .workbench-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 8vw, 5.25rem);
    line-height: 0.98;
    letter-spacing: -0.06em;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .lead {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 2vw, 1.35rem);
    line-height: 1.65;
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  .workbench-intro {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .workbench-intro h2,
  .example-note h2,
  .workbench-cta h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.6rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .workbench-intro p,
  .note-grid p,
  .example-note p,
  .workbench-cta p {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .workbench-intro p + p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .note-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .note-grid article,
  .example-note,
  .workbench-cta {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--color-bg-elevated);
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }

  .note-grid span {
    display: block;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .note-grid h2 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
  }

  .workbench-entries {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .workbench-entries > .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .workbench-entry {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--color-bg-elevated);
    padding: 2rem;
  }

  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .entry-date {
    display: block;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .entry-header h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    line-height: 1.08;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .entry-body p {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.75;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .entry-body p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .example-note {
    background:
      linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(110, 231, 183, 0.12), transparent),
      var(--color-bg-secondary);
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .workbench-cta {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .note-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .workbench-cta {
      align-items: flex-start;
      flex-direction: column;
    }
  }
</style>
